d it's never  going to stop because antonio brown's  not going to stop talking and if big ben  calls out you you nailed it big ben i go  back to uh this last offseason when they  draft mason rudolph and you have the  whole comments where where ben says you  know what it's not my job to teach him  i'm not going to be his mentor that's  leadership from within that's just not  there  and ben is a hall of fame quarterback  two-time super bowl champion but  sometimes your your best leader is not  your quarterback but you know they get  thrust into that situation and everyone  looks to them and i think that's where  the problem comes from is that your  coach and your quarterback are not  strong leaders in the fact that they can  tell everyone hey look we got to button  it up we have to keep everything in the  in the locker room here we can't be  going to the media we can't be going to  social media you need to have one or the  two and when when the quarterback and  the coach kind of put a blind eye to it  this is how this is how everyone starts  talking this is how you have this  carnival go on for what feels like a  couple years now  and i feel like the the standard that  applies to ben roethlisberger is the  simple result of the fact the steelers  went 20 years between terry bradshaw and  ben roethlisberger without a franchise  quarterback they didn't win a super bowl  during that stretch and they realize  we've got a guy who's one of the best  quarterbacks in the nfl maybe he doesn't  get the credit for being a great  quarterback that he should but he is a  great quarterback they put up with all  the other stuff because who's going to  be the quarterback and you see they've  been kind of clumsily flailing at trying  to find someone they can groom josh  dobbs one year mason rudolph the next  year who knows what they'll do in the  draft this year they have to be thinking  about life without ben roethlisberger  but until they get to the point where he  is done they got to find a way to make  it work and i feel like they're just  trying to hold it all together as best  they can while they limp to the finish  line of roethlisberger's career and try  to win one more super bowl and that's  just not the way to do it also one of  the other issues with the steelers and i  mentioned this from time to time i feel  like that they've allowed this hey we've  only had three coaches in 50 years to  become such a part of their identity  that they don't hold their current coach  accountable the way they should because  they still want to be able to say we've  only had three coaches in 51 years now  it's 52. now it's 53. look at us we're  going for three coaches in 60 years and  the coach feels like he's never going to  be run out the door because the the  organization takes so much pride in  never firing a coach that the coach can  say hey i don't have to worry about  getting fired  yeah no you're right and i i always  criticize teams that fire coaches too  quickly so i'm not gonna sit here and  say that you know the steelers going the  opposite direction is a bad thing i  think there's a fine balance where coach  can't feel like they're always coaching  for their job and saying hey this next  decision might be the enemy they have to  have the freedom to take risks and take  the correct risks but but at some point  you're right it gets to a point where  it's like mike tomlin can do anything  mike tomlin can have year after year  after year where they don't meet the  expectations and fall short and still  have a job so  you know teams teams do sometimes have  coaches too long i think about even  lovey smith with the bears you know  lovey smith was a good coach lovey smith  was consistent and the bears were good  year after year and uh at least you know  not great but they were in the hunt  and eventually got to a point where it's  like look it's just the same thing year  after year they haven't gotten to that  next level maybe we got to move on and 